guide-entry-nc-dermal = Dermal armor: manufacture and surgery
nc-guide-dermal-1 = [head=1]Dermal armor[/head]
    Skinweave provides SP 7 to the chest, abdomen and groin. Subdermal plates provide SP 11 to all body zones, including the head, eyes, mouth, arms, hands, legs and feet. One torso bay accepts either implant. Neither requires a battery or neural link; EMP does not disable them.
nc-guide-dermal-2 = [head=2]Manufacture[/head]
    Synthesize one biocompatible fiber from 5 oil + 10 carbon + 5 silicon at 350 K. Construction menu: 2 cyberfiber + 1 plastic + 2 cloth make one dermal mesh. Print dermal plates on the mechanical cyberware lathe (1800 steel + 200 plastic per set).
    Skinweave kit: 2 meshes + 1 plastic + 2 cloth. Plate kit: 1 mesh + 2 sets of plates + 2 plastic. Construction produces an unprepared implant. Use the cyberware workbench and sterile packaging to complete factory preparation before implantation.
nc-guide-dermal-3 = [head=2]Installation[/head]
    Stabilize the patient, prepare a sterile field and provide anesthesia. Make and retract local incisions at the dermal bay position (right side of the diagram) in the chest, abdomen and groin. Skinweave requires depth 0.28; plates require 0.38. Do not open bone.
    Mount the prepared implant in the chest dermal bay with the cyberware service tool. Distribute protection in order: chest, abdomen, groin. Return to the chest and secure the matrix. Plates additionally require an alignment check. Use the highlighted stage targets; a generic incision elsewhere is insufficient.
nc-guide-dermal-4 = [head=2]Completion and maintenance[/head]
    Protection remains inactive until every stage is complete. Close all incisions, check bleeding and infection, and inspect the cyberware tab of the body scanner. No neural calibration is required. Penetrations reduce SP and implant integrity; removal does not restore them. Use clinical cyberware servicing for repair. Worn armor and dermal armor use the highest applicable SP, never their sum.
